  "pathMultiplicationTip": "{table, select, 1{Multiplying by 1 changes nothing: 1 × 7 = 7.} 2{Multiplying by 2 means adding the number to itself: 2 × 6 = 6 + 6.} 3{The answers go up by 3 each time: 3, 6, 9, 12…} 4{Multiplying by 4 means doubling twice: 4 × 6 is double 12.} 5{The answers in the 5 times table always end in 0 or 5.} 6{6 times an even number ends with that same digit: 6 × 4 = 24.} 7{For 7 × 8, think 5, 6, 7, 8: 56 = 7 × 8.} 8{Multiplying by 8 means doubling three times: 8 × 3 = 24.} 9{The two digits of the answer always add up to 9: 9 × 4 = 36 and 3 + 6 = 9.} 10{Multiplying by 10 means adding a 0 on the right: 10 × 7 = 70.} 11{Up to 11 × 9, you write the digit twice: 11 × 4 = 44.} other{12 times a number is 10 times plus 2 times: 12 × 3 = 30 + 6.}}",
  "numbersUnitName": "{unit, select, count10{Count to 10} count20{Count to 20} count100{Count to 100} tensones{Tens and ones} compare{Compare} order{Put in order} beforeafter{Before and after} other{}}",
  "numbersUnitTip": "{unit, select, count10{Touch each mango as you count: one, two, three… The last number tells how many there are.} count20{First count the row of 10, then keep going: 11, 12, 13…} count100{A bundle is 10 sticks. Count the bundles in tens: 10, 20, 30…} tensones{The tens digit counts the bundles, the ones digit the loose sticks: 3 bundles and 4 sticks make 34.} compare{Look at the tens first: the number with more tens is greater. If the tens are the same, look at the ones.} order{On the line, numbers go from smallest to greatest. Find how much they go up each time.} beforeafter{The number after is 1 more; the number before is 1 less. After 39 comes 40!} other{}}",
  "divisionUnitName": "Divide by {number}",
  "divisionUnitTip": "{unit, select, 1{Dividing by 1 changes nothing: 7 ÷ 1 = 7.} 2{Dividing by 2 means sharing into two equal parts: 12 ÷ 2 = 6, half of 12.} 3{Dividing by 3 means making 3 equal parts: 12 ÷ 3 = 4, because 3 × 4 = 12.} 4{Dividing by 4 means halving twice: 20 ÷ 4 = 5, because half of 20 is 10 and half of 10 is 5.} 5{In the 5 times table, the numbers end in 0 or 5: 35 ÷ 5 = 7, because 5 × 7 = 35.} 6{For 42 ÷ 6, look in the 6 times table: 6 × 7 = 42, so 42 ÷ 6 = 7.} 7{For 56 ÷ 7, think of 7 × 8 = 56: so 56 ÷ 7 = 8.} 8{Dividing by 8 means halving three times: for 48 ÷ 8, 48 → 24 → 12 → 6.} 9{The digits of the numbers in the 9 times table add up to 9: 36 is 3 + 6 = 9, and 36 ÷ 9 = 4.} 10{Dividing by 10 means removing the 0 on the right: 70 ÷ 10 = 7.} 11{A number like 44 or 77 has two identical digits: 77 ÷ 11 = 7.} 12{Dividing by 12 means thinking 10 times plus 2 times: 36 ÷ 12 = 3, because 12 × 3 = 36.} other{}}",
